# Independent review — inactive request-owned v2 admission

Review base: `946a591`, as verified by the root agent before preparation. Reviewed `AGENTS.md`, the current verifier completion/next-task marker, the completed verifier, new admission source, completed v2 cleanup/proof/capacity transports, and the preserved exact capacity cleanup path. Only the new independent test file and this report were edited by this review agent.

## Result

No implementation blocker was found in the reviewed bounded preparation. The independent six-case suite passed on its first execution; its initial log is `review-tests-initial.log`. Formatting and scoped ESLint pass (`review-format-initial.log`, `review-lint.log`). Full aggregate tests/types/build and actual isolated SQL composition are separate root/SQL-agent evidence.

## Source review

The factory returns one request-owned, one-shot handle. It captures required trusted methods and validates proof-age configuration; no Auth/store fallback is installed. The handle exists before admission begins, allowing `close()` to fence pending verification or registration synchronously. Ownership is allocated and frozen before any cleanup write can run. Initial verification failure does not invent remote ownership or cleanup effects.

The claim lifetime is the minimum of actual lifecycle ceilings, excluding the verifier's 30000-ms freshness deadline. The original claim remains immutable. Later Auth/allowlist/approval observations and cleanup effective expiry only shrink the retained local ceiling. Owner, actual Auth session, parent version, fingerprint, exact approval identity and immutable issuance/envelope facts stay pinned across verifications.

Each deliberate claim or inspect creates one immutable full protocol-2 proof from that verification's conservative original start. The configured proof age is 1..30000 ms. Post-registration and post-consumption verification are bounded by the same original proof deadline; they cannot refresh its start or expiry. A shorter lifecycle observation after registration denies that operation instead of rewriting its proof or generating a replacement. Every proof registration and consuming acknowledgment matches the entire retained request, including original times and exact cleanup/claim association.

Initial success requires combined capacity claim followed by a distinct existing-claim inspect using another fresh proof. No base-only admission, lazy capacity attachment, duplicate receipt consumption or legacy evidence peeling is introduced. Overlapping same-handle revalidation returns false without allocating another proof. Different handles have isolated ownership, clocks, minimum ceilings, operation arrays and terminal state.

Close and terminal invalidation synchronously clear the retained envelope reference. Protected verification wrappers check terminal state/time before and after each awaited port. Late exact registration or consumption acknowledgments remain content-free evidence but cannot reactivate a closed/invalid handle. Failure does not automatically close, refund, retry registration or replace identities. Explicit close uses the exact retained capacity-cleanup identity and remains available after Auth loss, expiry or clock failure; uncertain cleanup can be deliberately retried only with that same identity.

Snapshots are frozen and content-free. They retain immutable ownership and ordered exact operation evidence with attempted/acknowledged state, without the envelope, authority/writing, credentials or raw failures. Snapshot state is only the last local observation: an `active` snapshot can age without revalidation and is never proof of fresh eligibility or dispatch permission. Shape validation and these local handles do not establish genuine Auth, service provenance, transport authenticity or provider permission. The supplied server boundary still owns those requirements.

## Independent tests

New file: `src/lib/ai/conversation/__tests__/p5-voice-v2-admission-review.test.ts`.

1. Cleanup ownership exists before its awaited registration; synchronous close wins and a late matching acknowledgment never starts proof/claim work or reopens the handle.
2. Closing while proof registration waits retains the late exact registration acknowledgment without consuming it.
3. An exact claim acknowledgment arriving at the original proof expiry remains recorded as uncertain evidence; no automatic inspect/close runs, and deliberate exact cleanup still succeeds without Auth or a working clock.
4. An overlapping revalidation creates no extra proof; a pending inspect cannot resurrect a handle closed during its wait, and all three deliberate operations use distinct receipts.
5. Two handles using the same trusted ports retain distinct claim/cleanup identities; closing one during registration leaves the other able to remain active and revalidate.
6. A fresh post-registration verifier starting later cannot refresh the original proof expiry; no consumption occurs at that deadline, while the longer immutable lifecycle expiry remains recorded.

All six use preserved real local AES-GCM/HMAC and the completed verifier, with explicitly fictional Auth/protected records. These are controlled local asynchronous orderings, not native cross-backend SQL concurrency, HTTP fault injection, deployed Auth/revocation delivery or provider/device acceptance. Actual isolated SQL composition is reported separately. No existing source, test, SQL, policy, setting or runtime route was changed by this review.

## Next bounded dependency

The remaining next dependency is admission-owned v2 marker orchestration. Extend the same trusted v2 ownership boundary so an active handle can make at most one marker attempt: retain one immutable operation ID and expected pool version before any wait, generate a separate fresh inspect proof exclusively for the completed full v2 marker transport, and preserve full late/uncertain acknowledgments without retry or replacement identity. It must use the actual private claim/cleanup ownership, never adopt a caller-supplied snapshot or pass through a consuming inspect first. Synchronous close, original proof timing, pinned association, shrink-only lifecycle and retained accounting remain mandatory. Keep provider calls, real Auth/device acceptance, runtime wiring and coordinated no-fallback route/privilege cutover deferred.

