# Inactive request-owned v2 admission — isolated SQL composition

Owned file: `src/lib/ai/conversation/__tests__/p5-voice-v2-admission-sql.test.ts` only. Existing code, tests, SQL, migrations and settings were unchanged by this test task.

First focused run: 41/41 pass, zero skips. Expanded focused run: **43/43 pass**, zero skips. The two additional cases cover a cleanup acknowledgment delayed to its original 30,000-ms freshness boundary and a committed claim reply arriving at its original proof expiry. No implementation, SQL or fixture correction was needed to make these suites pass; new checks were added without relaxing existing assertions. Initial logs remain preserved.

The fixture composes real unchanged local P3b AES-GCM/HMAC, the completed v2 verifier, actual protected parent/current/cleanup/proof/combined-capacity-claim/inspect transports, and the existing exact `bfa_p5_capacity_close` transport. It applies preserved SQL drafts only to isolated PostgreSQL 18.6 through PGlite 0.5.12. Auth/policy/approval inputs remain explicitly fictional. No marker or provider operation occurs.

Coverage includes:

- Full success before marker creation, one quota debit, immutable claim/cleanup/Auth association and exact distinct consumed claim/inspect proofs.
- A 60-second lifecycle revalidated after 40 seconds, proving that original verifier freshness is not substituted for lifecycle expiry; every inspect uses its own fresh proof.
- Repeated inspection without another debit, same-attempt competing handle denial, and winner isolation.
- Parent/approval revocation, thread revision change, allowlist withdrawal and Auth-session change after cleanup registration, proof registration, capacity claim and inspect commit.
- Lost or protocol-mixed replies at all four write/consume stages with exact retained ownership. Lost claim/inspect outcomes retain occupied slots and cumulative debit until deliberate close; no automatic close or retry is inferred.
- Proof expiry and shortened current eligibility after registration, shrink-only ceilings across later Auth/policy extension, late cleanup and late committed claim acknowledgment.
- Synchronous close before delayed cleanup/claim SQL, close during a delayed committed claim reply, close before initial Auth completes, and explicit same-identity close retry after lost acknowledgment even when Auth is foreign and local time is unsafe.
- Concurrent JavaScript handles keep their owner/session/claim/proof associations isolated. Complete transactions are explicitly serialized on the one local PGlite connection; this is not native database concurrency evidence.

A 22-table snapshot preserves all pre-existing records except the expected target quota, pool and safe owner-clock changes. This includes unrelated same-owner historical holds plus private saved fictional text. Success and deliberate close preserve cumulative usage while releasing only the target unmarked slot. All other tests independently assert target accounting and exact retained cleanup identities.

Limits: local timing/response hooks are not HTTP fault injection, real Auth delivery, native SQL scheduling, provider finality, device acceptance or runtime wiring. Both local engines close, original SQL clock behavior and Date mocks restore, and no connected service or real participant is used.

Evidence: `admission-sql-initial.log/json`, `admission-sql-confirmed.log/json`, and scoped typecheck/lint logs. Existing runner limits remain `--maxWorkers=2 --testTimeout=15000`.

=== admission-implementation-notes.md ===
# Inactive v2 request-owned admission implementation

New owned files only:
- `src/lib/ai/conversation/service/voice-v2-admission.server.ts`
- `src/lib/ai/conversation/__tests__/p5-voice-v2-admission.test.ts`

`createVoiceV2Admission(VoiceV2AdmissionPorts)` returns a synchronous request-owned handle exposing one-shot `admit(raw): Promise<boolean>`, deliberate `revalidate(): Promise<boolean>`, synchronous-fencing `close(): Promise<boolean>`, and content-free `snapshot()`. The handle exists before the initial Auth wait, so cancellation can fence pending admission. Constructor captures bound trusted methods/config once. The factory and return object contain no external client, credential, default Auth, fallback, marker or provider operation.

Admit verifies using the completed separate v2 verifier and real preserved crypto, pins exact original owner/session/parent/fingerprint/issuance/approval, then allocates one immutable claim and cleanup pair before the first possible write. The original claim uses the minimum lifecycle component deadlines, explicitly excluding the verifier's 30000-ms freshness ceiling. Cleanup registration retains its request before its one invocation and requires the complete matching protocol-2 association, nonterminal fields and a valid no-larger effective deadline. Freshness of this request remains bound to its original verifier start across the wait.

Initial admission uses one fresh full claim proof and combined capacity claim, followed by a separate fresh full inspect proof and existing-claim-only inspect. Each deliberate revalidation uses exactly one further inspect proof. Each operation retains its immutable request before its registration call. Complete registration ACK and complete consumption ACK must match every proof identity/time field. Fresh post-registration and post-consumption verification runs with the operation's original proof deadline as an additional bound; those newer verifier starts never rewrite or refresh the existing proof. Dynamic evidence can only shorten the handle's lifecycle ceilings. If a later shorter ceiling cannot support an already-registered immutable proof, the operation denies without a replacement receipt or consumption retry.

Per-handle monotonic clock high-water, original immutable claim, actual Auth association and exact approval remain pinned across all checks. A new deliberate verification after 30 seconds can create a new proof if the original lifecycle remains eligible, but cannot renew the retained claim or erase earlier shorter evidence. Different handles share no local ownership or timing state. Same-handle overlapping revalidate returns false without new work; close synchronously sets terminal state and clears the opaque request reference before any cleanup await. Late matching cleanup/proof/consumption ACKs may be retained as evidence but cannot restore eligibility.

An operational failure returns false, terminally invalidates local work, clears the request and retains all exact content-free ownership/attempted ACK evidence. It does not automatically close, retry, refund, recreate identity or infer rollback. Explicit close uses only the previously owned exact cleanup/claim through the existing dedicated cleanup path, independently of current Auth, time or allowlist. Concurrent close calls coalesce; an uncertain close can be explicitly retried using the same exact ownership. Close before any ownership is local closure only: no write can have started, and pending verification cannot subsequently allocate/register.

Snapshots contain protocol 2, last local state, exact ownership/cleanup request/ACK, lifecycle ceiling and immutable ordered proof/consumption evidence. No envelope, authority body, writing, key or raw error is returned. `active` is a last observed local state that can age without revalidation; neither it nor a successful admission is fresh provider permission. Finite Auth/SQL checks are not an atomic lock spanning providers. Marker orchestration, deployed Auth/revocation policy, provider/device acceptance, runtime wiring and quiesced no-fallback cutover remain deferred.

The design initially considered automatic cleanup on failure, but comparison with the existing bridge and independent review retained explicit-only cleanup before implementation. No automatic cleanup implementation or failing test resulted. No existing application source, parser/seal, SQL, migration, test, dependency or configuration was changed.

Validation: primary 77/77 on first run (`admission-primary-initial.log`), scoped implementation/primary-test ESLint passes (`admission-primary-lint.log`). No initial primary failures or assertions weakened. Independent source review and six additional checks pass; actual isolated SQL first 41/41 and expanded 43/43 pass, with logs/notes owned by those agents. SQL agent reports strict TypeScript pass on completed files. Root runs final aggregate checks after freeze. Final source addition after primary run was documentation-only: snapshot active describes last local observation, not fresh permission.

Tests use actual local P3b AES-GCM/HMAC with a fictional secret and explicit fictional Auth/current/clock records; SQL suite composes actual existing transports and isolated PostgreSQL with those fictional boundaries. No real Auth/model/voice calls, native concurrent backend acceptance, HTTP fault injection, provider/device acceptance or connected SQL is claimed.
